<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palar River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palar is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, crosses a corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and flows east through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. Its valley, with Kanchipuram and many temple towns, was the heartland of the Pallava kingdom and later of Vijayanagara's northern Tamil provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palar-river.jpg" alt="Palar River" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PlaneMad · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mouth_of_the_Palar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Palar means milk river in Tamil. The river flows 93 kilometres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and 222 kilometres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, with a shorter stretch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> at Vayalur, about 75 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. For a long distance it runs underground, emerging near Bethamangala, from where it gathers water and flows east down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>. Of its seven tributaries the chief is the Cheyyar River.</p><p>Among the towns on its banks are Kuppam, Vaniyambadi, Ambur, Gudiyatham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a>, Katpadi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>, Ranipet, Walajapet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, Chengalpattu and Kalpakkam. Rainfall across the basin is low and droughts are frequent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sharing of its water was settled in 1892, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> were part of the Madras Presidency and the upper basin lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> kingdom; the Palar was one of the rivers named in Schedule A of that agreement. A plan by the Andhra Pradesh government for an irrigation dam at Ganeshpuram near Kuppam caused agitation in the northern districts of Tamil Nadu.</p><p>The former Chief Minister Jayalalitha opposed it, arguing that under the 1892 agreement an upstream state should build no new dam to obstruct, divert or store the river's water without the downstream state's consent. In 2008 the documentary En Peyar Palar, directed by R.R. Srinivasan, traced the harm done to the river by sand quarrying and industrial effluents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Water diverted at the Palar anicut feeds the Poondi reservoir and Chembarambakkam Lake, two major sources of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>'s supply, though the Telugu Ganga project, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> water to the city, has much reduced the dependence on the Palar. Karnataka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> have built many minor and medium irrigation tanks in the basin. In Karnataka the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> of Bethamangala, long the main source of drinking water for the Kolar Gold Fields, and Ramasagara lie within seven kilometres of each other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palar River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1509 one line, traditionally founded by Tubaki Krishnappa Nayaka, was installed at Gingee (Senji), controlling the land between the <b>Palar</b> and Kollidam rivers, including the coast around Pondicherry.</blockquote><a href="/state/gingee-nayaks">Gingee Nayaks</a></li></ul></section>
